BODY = """
<h3>Why mantle cell lymphoma is managed in two tracks</h3>
<p>Mantle cell lymphoma ranges from indolent to highly aggressive disease, and most patients are older at diagnosis. Care has therefore developed along two tracks, intensive therapy for fit patients and gentler therapy for older or less fit patients, with a third track for relapse. Biology cuts across all three: Ki-67 emerged early as the main prognostic factor [[Nordic MCL2 (Geisler)]], and TP53 alterations now define the group least served by any approach.</p>

<h3>Fit patients: intensification, then de-escalation</h3>
<p>The first randomized evidence that consolidation matters came from the European MCL Network, where autologous transplant in first remission more than doubled median PFS compared with interferon (39 vs 17 months) [[European MCL Network (Dreyling)]]. Two intensive strategies followed. In the US, R-HyperCVAD/MA without transplant produced complete responses in 87%, but with 8% treatment-related deaths [[R-HyperCVAD/MA (Romaguera, MDACC)]]. In Europe, the Nordic group showed that cytarabine-containing induction followed by transplant gave long remissions, with a plateau in some patients [[Nordic MCL2 (Geisler)]], and MCL Younger confirmed that alternating R-CHOP and R-DHAP before transplant more than doubled time to treatment failure (median 9.1 vs 3.9 years) [[MCL Younger (Hermine)]]. LyMa then made rituximab maintenance after transplant standard, the first post-transplant step to improve survival (4-year overall survival 89% vs 80%) [[LyMa (Le Gouill)]].</p>
<p>BTK inhibitors have since reopened the question of how much intensity is needed. TRIANGLE added ibrutinib to induction and maintenance: 3-year failure-free survival was 88% with ibrutinib plus transplant and 86% with ibrutinib without transplant, against 72% with the standard transplant arm [[TRIANGLE (Dreyling)]]. In E4151, patients in MRD-negative first remission had the same 3-year survival with or without transplant (82.1% vs 82.7%) [[ECOG-ACRIN E4151]]. WINDOW-1 showed that a BTK inhibitor-first approach can shorten the chemotherapy needed [[WINDOW-1 (Wang, MDACC)]].</p>

<h3>Older patients: from R-CHOP to bendamustine and BTK inhibitors</h3>
<p>MCL Elderly established R-CHOP over R-FC and showed that rituximab maintenance after R-CHOP improved survival (4-year overall survival 87% vs 63%) [[MCL Elderly (Kluin-Nelemans)]]. Bendamustine-rituximab then became the most widely used backbone: in StiL NHL1 the MCL subgroup had a median PFS of 35.4 vs 22.1 months with less toxicity than R-CHOP [[StiL NHL1 (Rummel)]], and BRIGHT confirmed it internationally while long-term data flagged more second cancers [[BRIGHT (Flinn)]]. Bortezomib-based VR-CAP improved both PFS and overall survival over R-CHOP but saw limited uptake [[LYM-3002 (Robak)]].</p>
<p>Adding a BTK inhibitor to bendamustine-rituximab improved PFS in two phase 3 trials: ibrutinib in SHINE (median 80.6 vs 52.9 months), with more toxicity and no survival gain [[SHINE (Wang)]], and acalabrutinib in ECHO (66.4 vs 49.6 months; survival HR 0.86, not significant), which led to approval in January 2025 [[ECHO (Wang)]]. ENRICH tested dropping chemotherapy altogether: ibrutinib-rituximab improved PFS overall, clearly against R-CHOP (HR 0.37) but not against bendamustine-rituximab (HR 0.91) [[ENRICH (UK)]].</p>

<h3>Chemotherapy-free and time-limited frontline therapy</h3>
<p>Phase 2 studies have tested whether chemotherapy is needed at all. Lenalidomide-rituximab gave a 92% response rate and 85% 2-year PFS [[R2 frontline (Ruan)]]. Ibrutinib, venetoclax and obinutuzumab produced complete metabolic responses in 86.6% of untreated patients, with a 5-year PFS of 80% in the 15 untreated patients (ASH 2024) [[OASIS]], and an MRD-guided acalabrutinib-lenalidomide-anti-CD20 approach gave a 4-year PFS of 76% [[MRD-driven acalabrutinib-lenalidomide-anti-CD20]]. These approaches are especially relevant for TP53-altered disease, where chemotherapy works poorly.</p>

<h3>Relapsed disease: the BTK inhibitor era and beyond</h3>
<p>Before BTK inhibitors, relapse options were modest: bortezomib (response rate 33%, the first MCL-specific approval) [[PINNACLE (Fisher)]], temsirolimus [[Temsirolimus (Hess)]] and lenalidomide [[EMERGE (Goy)]] [[SPRINT (Trněný)]]. Ibrutinib changed this, with a 68% response rate [[PCYC-1104 (Wang)]] and more than double the PFS of temsirolimus in RAY (14.6 vs 6.2 months) [[RAY (Dreyling)]]; its US MCL indication was later withdrawn (2023) when confirmatory requirements were not met. The more selective acalabrutinib (response rate 81%) [[ACE-LY-004 (Wang)]] and zanubrutinib (84%) [[BGB-3111-206 (Song)]] followed.</p>
<p>After a covalent BTK inhibitor, prognosis was historically poor, and four strategies now address it. The noncovalent BTK inhibitor pirtobrutinib produced responses in 57.8% [[BRUIN (Wang)]]. CD19 CAR-T gave complete responses in 67% with brexucabtagene autoleucel [[ZUMA-2 (Wang)]] and 72.3% with lisocabtagene maraleucel [[TRANSCEND NHL-001, MCL cohort]]. BCL2 inhibition, first combined with ibrutinib in AIM [[AIM (Tam)]], improved PFS in SYMPATICO (31.9 vs 22.1 months) [[SYMPATICO (Wang)]], and sonrotoclax received accelerated approval in May 2026 after BTK inhibitors [[Sonrotoclax (BCL2 inhibitor)]]. The CD20×CD3 bispecific glofitamab produced complete responses in 78.3% [[Glofitamab (Phillips)]], although it is not FDA-approved in MCL.</p>

<h3>Where we are now</h3>
<p>Published summaries of NCCN version 4.2026 describe the following; individual placements were not checked against the NCCN document itself.</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Fit patients without TP53 alteration:</b> the TRIANGLE ibrutinib-containing regimen is preferred, with acalabrutinib or zanubrutinib as possible substitutes. Patients in MRD-negative complete remission may omit transplant and proceed to rituximab maintenance with or without a BTK inhibitor.</li>
<li><b>Older or less fit patients:</b> bendamustine-rituximab with or without acalabrutinib, or acalabrutinib-rituximab.</li>
<li><b>TP53-altered disease:</b> clinical trials are strongly encouraged; a BTK inhibitor-venetoclax-obinutuzumab triplet is an option.</li>
<li><b>Relapse:</b> a covalent BTK inhibitor if not used before; after BTK inhibitor failure, pirtobrutinib, CD19 CAR-T or a BCL2 inhibitor-based approach.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Open questions</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Transplant:</b> which fit patients still benefit, beyond those in MRD-negative remission?</li>
<li><b>Duration:</b> can BTK inhibitor-based therapy be stopped, guided by MRD?</li>
<li><b>Survival:</b> BTK inhibitor plus chemotherapy improves PFS without a survival gain so far; is chemotherapy-free therapy as good or better?</li>
<li><b>Sequencing after BTK inhibitors:</b> CAR-T, bispecifics, pirtobrutinib and BCL2 inhibitors have not been compared head to head.</li>
<li><b>TP53:</b> no approach yet gives durable control for most patients.</li>
</ul>

<h3>What is coming</h3>
<p>Ongoing phase 3 trials (listed under Trials to watch at the end of this page) test zanubrutinib-rituximab against bendamustine-rituximab, continuous versus intermittent zanubrutinib, orelabrutinib with bendamustine-rituximab, pirtobrutinib against covalent BTK inhibitors, sonrotoclax with zanubrutinib, glofitamab against investigator's choice (GLOBRYTE) and rocbrutinib against BTK inhibitors.</p>
"""
